         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"unitaryCircle\" id=\"circtri0\"><style>.circle-draw circle, .circle-draw line {stroke: #71a6d2; stroke-width: 4; stroke-linecap: round; fill: none;} .circle-draw circle { stroke-dasharray: 942; stroke-dashoffset: 942; animation: drawCircle 2s linear forwards;} .radius-line { transform-origin: 300px 170px; animation: rotateRadius 2s linear forwards;} .radius-45 { opacity: 0; animation: showRadius45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .radius-30{ opacity: 0; animation: showRadius30 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .vertical45 { opacity: 0; animation: showVertical45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.5s; } .atlasTextRadius { opacity: 0; animation: atlasTextRadiusMov 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.8s; }</style><style>@keyframes drawCircle { to {stroke-dashoffset: 0; } } @keyframes rotateRadius { from { transform: rotate(0deg); } to   { transform: rotate(-360deg); } } @keyframes showRadius45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showRadius30 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showVertical45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }  @keyframes atlasTextRadiusMov { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }</style><div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 340\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 320 147 A 30 30 0 0 1 340.0 170.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"470\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"8\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"170\" r=\"148\" transform=\"scale(-1 1) translate(-600 0)  rotate(180 300 170)\"></circle> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"448\" y2=\"169\"></line> <line class=\"radius-45\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"406\" y2=\"66\"></line>  <line class=\"vertical45\" x1=\"406\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"406\" y2=\"166\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"188\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"88\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">a</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"390\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">b</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"320\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"1.0em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"9\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">y</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"407\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"405\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">Q</text>  <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"463\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">x</text></svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 1</b>: Circle with unit radius <b>c</b> containing a right triangle.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Walking on the Circle\n\nAfter studying how to locate ourselves using a point on the circle (<htcite0>), this text begins to examine what happens to the trigonometric functions as we walk around the circle.\n\nThe previous text also showed that the square of the cosine plus the square of the sine always equals 1 (<htcite1>) — which leads us to a reflection:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b49ab1bf-e2ad-4c82-a38b-1725cfa9d03f",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Where are we on a disk?",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b49ab1bf-e2ad-4c82-a38b-1725cfa9d03f",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Where are we on a disk?",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Before we plot</b><br /><br />What would the graphs of the cosine and sine functions look like in the first quadrant of a circle?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Cosine Values\n\nFor a clearer understanding of the cosine function, read the text dedicated to it (<htcite0>). That text defines the function as:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4c95f9bf-befa-42bd-8d56-0cf962e09c14",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Trigonometry (Cosine)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>cos (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>adjacent leg</mi><mi>hypotenuse</mi></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Definition of the cosine of theta (θ).</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "From **Formula 1**, we can observe that as the hypotenuse moves away from the horizontal axis — that is, as we raise it — the projection on the horizontal axis decreases until it reaches zero, when the hypotenuse is completely vertical. This means that the cosine starts at its maximum (1) and decreases to its minimum (0), as shown in **Chart 1**.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chartcos0\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 1</b>: Chart of the cosine function for the first quadrant of the circle. Chart created with the <htcite0> library.</div></p><script> htPlotSineCosine('chartcos0', true, false, 1);</script>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Sine Values\n\nAs with the cosine function, we recommend reading the text dedicated to the sine function (<htcite0>) before continuing. That text defines the function as:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "341c9ef9-9320-4c24-8aa4-a533a3290d8a",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Trigonometry (Sine)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>sin (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>opposite leg</mi><mi>hypotenuse</mi></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 2</b>: Definition of the sine of theta (θ).</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "**Formula 2** tells us that as the hypotenuse approaches the vertical axis — that is, as we raise it — the projection on the vertical axis increases until it reaches 1, when the hypotenuse is completely vertical. This means that the sine starts at its minimum (0) and rises to its maximum (1), as shown in **Chart 2**.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chartsin0\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 2</b>: Chart of the sine function for the first quadrant of the circle. Chart created with the <htcite0> library.</div></p><script> htPlotSineCosine('chartsin0', false, true, 1);</script>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Sum of Values\n\nComparing **Charts 1 and 2**, we can see that as the cosine values decrease, the sine of the angle increases. The previous text (<htcite0>) showed that the squares of the two functions follow **Formula 3**:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b49ab1bf-e2ad-4c82-a38b-1725cfa9d03f",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Where are we on a disk?",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>cos&sup2; (θ) + sin&sup2; (θ)</mn><mn>=</mn><mi>1</mi></math></p> <p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 3</b>: Fundamental trigonometric identity.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "To see how these quantities relate, **Chart 3** combines the results of all three formulas:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chartcossin\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 3</b>: Chart of the sine and cosine functions and the trigonometric identity that relates them in the first quadrant of the circle. Chart created with the <htcite0> library.</div></p><script> htPlotSineCosine('chartcossin', true, true, 1);</script>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: What about the rest of the circle?</b><br /><br />All of these charts use only a quarter of the circle. Do the formulas also work for the rest of the circle?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "We may be tempted to confirm right now that everything we studied also works for the rest of the circle. For now, though, the priority is to understand how everything works before generalizing. So we simply state that everything presented here is also valid for the other three quadrants, and we will return to them when the time is right.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
